Z||000011||Getaway2k||11-05-2000||11:23 AM||||The best gas mileage I've seen with my 86 GT Fastback is 17.5 mpg. ||||reg||1
Z||000012||theogre||11-05-2000||11:38 AM||||If the TPS is more than a few years old consider just replacing it. It does wear internally and can get contaminated both of which will throw it out of whack. You can test the thing but the test doesn't give you an acurate picture of condition. It just tells you the thing is working not how well it works.<P>Vacumm leaks or problems with EGR will get you. These won't always throw codes. same with exhaust crack which throw off the O2 sensor.||||reg||3
Z||000013||mshill||11-05-2000||12:10 PM||||I keep thinking that my gas mileage is bad at about 18-19 mpg (50/50 city/highway) with a GM Performance Cam.  Keep in mind also that I am in California where we have that MTBE poison in the gas that cuts your gas mileage by 10% (so I have heard).  I still think that I have something amiss even at that.  I have heard of people getting 25 mpg in the city with the V6.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000014||Eric Marsh||11-05-2000||12:24 PM||||I was wondering why my gas mileage was poor and eventually pulled my tank and found the problem. Talk about a gas sucking monster:<P>[IMG] <A HREF="http://www.hotrodder.com/emarsh/images/gas-monster.jpg" TARGET=_blank>http://www.hotrodder.com/emarsh/images/gas-monster.jpg</A>  [/IMG]||||reg||1
Z||000015||ur2biteme||11-05-2000||04:39 PM||||when i got my car back in sept.  '98, the car only got about 15-17 mpg, divided between freeway and city.  the car had an internaly shot muffler (looked like it tried to explode, puffed out on all sides) bad sensors all around, and a fouled out cat converter.  <P>well, the converter wasnt plugged, so i fixed everything else, O2, tps, iac valve, new coils(87 duke), crank sensor(87 duke), flowmaster muffler, you get the picture.  I also had a fuel line leaking as well.  that caused most of the bad mileage.  <P>before my tranny died, after these repairs, i got about 25mpg in town (with a lead foot) and about 32-35 freeway, cruzing between 80-85.  i've even babied it and got over 40 mpg on the freeway with an engine pushing 170k.  <P>there is no reason for that kind of fuel economy out of either the 4 or 6.  there is definitely something wrong.  more than likely, one of the many things i wen through.  lol<P>------------------<BR>UR2BITEME<BR>'87 econo coupe<BR>170k and a bad fender :(||||reg||1
Z||000016||theogre||11-05-2000||04:51 PM||||Hags, what year 4 is that and what code is in the ECM? Any code in the ECM will screw up milage. The code may tell you what's having a fit. (Don't just go by the book, the codes can be misleading.)||||reg||1
Z||000017||Mark||11-05-2000||06:31 PM||||An arguement for "poor" mileage....<P>If I remember correctly, the V6 engine cars had an EPA city fuel mileage estimate of something like 19 or 20 mpg.  It has been my experience that matching the manufacturers estimate, which is derived under IDEAL conditions, is very unlikely.  One "real world" condition is warm up.  You can bet the mileage isn't anywhere close to the EPA estimate until the engine is fully warmed up Also, popular modifications, like low-temp thermostats will likely reduce mileage as well. <P>An arguement for "great" gas mileage...<P>When I fill up, I can squeeze almost one additional gallon of gas in the tank after the pump auto-shut off.  Adding in this extra gas or not  can make a huge difference in my calculated mileage, since I'm usually buying only 7 or 8 gallons.  If I don't squeeze in that extra gas, I'll get some really "great" mileage numbers.  However, if I really top up the tank, the city mileage is usually around 19 or 20. The bottom line is calculate your mileage over several tanks of gas, and be careful how you fill it.||||reg||1
Z||000018||88formula||11-05-2000||07:03 PM||||EPA mileage for the v6 5-speed formula is 17 city and 27 highway. I still have the factory window sticker.||||reg||1
Z||000019||roadrunner||11-05-2000||08:41 PM||||I drive in Atlanta rush hour traffic and average 20 to 22 miles per gallon on an '86 V6 automatic.  The driving is mostly at 45 mph with stops at traffic lights.  On the interstate, i get 24 miles per gallon at 70 to 75 miles per hour.  I had an '87 GT which got 28 miles per gallon on the interstate at 65 to 70 miles per hour with a V6 automatic and 18 miles per gallon in Atlanta rush hour traffic.   The '86 is more consistent, but I can't get the interstate mileage.  Go figure!||||reg||1
